.title = "Choose an API",
.description = "Choose the Zig package, C ABI, a language binding, the CLI, or browser WebAssembly without changing the CapTP protocol.",
.date = .date("2026-06-12T00:00:00"),
.layout = "page.shtml",
.draft = false,
---

glowcap has one protocol implementation and several APIs. Choose the one that
matches your language and runtime.

[One core under every API, so the entry point you choose does not change what a session puts on the wire.]($image.siteAsset('diagram-surfaces.svg'))

| API | You write | Reach for it when |
| --- | --- | --- |
| [Zig package]($link.page('start')) | Zig | You own allocators and I/O, and you want the engine types in the same language. |
| [C ABI](https://github.com/jimisaacs/glowcap/blob/main/docs/consuming-from-c.md) | C / any FFI | You are wrapping a language with no binding, or you already own the event loop and sockets. |
| [Bindings]($link.page('bindings')) | Rust, TypeScript, Python, Racket | You want `async`/`await` (or Racket threads) and do not want to pump bytes. |
| [CapTP CLI]($link.page('cli')) | shell or MCP | You want to call or host from a terminal, or expose CapTP tools to an editor assistant via MCP. |

The TypeScript binding also compiles to WebAssembly, so the same session runs in
the browser ([In the browser]($link.page('bindings/browser'))).

# One engine

A Zig peer, a C consumer, and any binding hold the same OCapN conversation byte
for byte. Which codec and CapTP version you speak comes from negotiation with
the partner, not from which API you use
([Concepts]($link.page('concepts'))).
